// ADR-038 D7, question 2, first rule: a value that may *not* cross a thread,
// handed to a thread the foreign runtime owns.
//
// `local_handle` returns a value holding an `Rc`, and it is **the foreign
// crate's** `Rc` rather than anything this language writes. That sentence used
// to read *"which is what `Shared` lowers to at `user_parallelism = no`
// (ADR-037 D3), and which `Shared` being unbuilt is the reason this experiment
// has to borrow from the foreign crate"*, and both halves are gone:
// ADR-037 D6 narrowed D3 so `Shared` is an atomic count at **both** settings,
// and `Shared` is built.
//
// **The experiment still has to borrow, and now for a sharper reason.** A
// Nikaia `Shared` cannot be written into this shape at all: ADR-061 D1 refuses
// one handed to code nothing describes, because D7's per-value inference makes
// a `Shared[Conn]` an `Rc` for one value and an `Arc` for another in the same
// program, and no foreign signature can name both. So the only way to build a
// value that may not cross and hand it to a foreign thread is to get it from
// the foreign side, which is what this does.
//
// **This program must not build.** It is kept because what it says when it
// fails is the finding: who refuses it, in whose vocabulary, and pointing at
// which file.
//
// All three answers changed at 0.0.161. It used to be `rustc`'s `Send` bound,
// translated onto the line below by ADR-005 D7 - the place right and the words
// Rust's. It is `NK2502` now, in this compiler's vocabulary, because the
// description says two things no signature could: `crosses = false` on
// `LocalHandle` and `threads = true` on the function (ADR-193 D1, D2).
fn main() {
    let handle = hyper_shim::local_handle("not Send")
    let crossed = hyper_shim::across_a_thread(handle)
    println(f"crossed: {crossed}")
}
